  "answer": "When the concentration of one of the reactants is increased in a system at chemical equilibrium, the equilibrium will shift in the direction that tends to reduce the concentration of the added reactant. This is known as Le Chatelier's principle. As a result, the reaction will proceed in the forward direction, consuming some of the added reactant and some of the products, until a new equilibrium is established. The concentration of the products will increase, while the concentration of the reactants will decrease, but not to their original levels. The system will reach a new equilibrium state.",
  "reasoning": "Le Chatelier's principle"
