  "answer": "The car will slow down and eventually stop. This is because the force of friction acts in the opposite direction to the motion of the car, and when it is greater than the force applied by the engine, it results in a net force opposing the motion. According to Newton's first law of motion, an object in motion will stay in motion unless acted upon by an unbalanced force. In this case, the unbalanced force (friction) causes the car to decelerate. To maintain motion, the driver should increase the force applied by the engine to overcome the frictional force.",
  "reasoning": "The question requires the student to evaluate the situation by analyzing the forces acting on the car and making a recommendation based on their understanding of forces and motion. This aligns with the 'Evaluate' level of Bloom's taxonomy and DOK3, as it involves making a judgment and justifying a course of action."
}', '{"question": "A car is moving forward on a straight road. If the force of friction is greater than the force applied by the engine, what will happen to the car? Evaluate the situation and recommend a course of action to maintain motion.", "answer": "The car will slow down and eventually stop. This is because the force of friction acts in the opposite direction to the motion of the car, and when it is greater than the force applied by the engine, it results in a net force opposing the motion. According to Newton's first law of motion, an object in motion will stay in motion unless acted upon by an unbalanced force. In this case, the unbalanced force (friction) causes the car to decelerate. To maintain motion, the driver should increase the force applied by the engine to overcome the frictional force.", "reasoning": "The question requires the student to evaluate the situation by analyzing the forces acting on the car and making a recommendation based on their understanding of forces and motion. This aligns with the 'Evaluate' level of Bloom's taxonomy and DOK3, as it involves making a judgment and justifying a course of action."}']

