['{
  "question": "Recall the formula for calculating power. Use this formula to determine the power output of a 50 kg student climbing a 10 m ladder in 5 seconds. Explain how the power output would change if the time was doubled.",
  "answer": "The formula for power is P = W/t, where W is work done and t is time. Work done W = mgh = 50 kg \u00d7 9.8 m/s\u00b2 \u00d7 10 m = 4900 J. Power P = 4900 J / 5 s = 980 W. If the time is doubled to 10 seconds, the power output would be P = 4900 J / 10 s = 490 W. Therefore, the power output decreases as the time increases.",
  "reasoning": "The question requires recalling the formula for power and applying it in a scenario. The power output is calculated as P = W/t, where W is work done and t is time. Work done W = mgh = 50 kg \u00d7 9.8 m/s\u00b2 \u00d7 10 m = 4900 J. Power P = 4900 J / 5 s = 980 W. If the time is doubled to 10 seconds, the power output would be P = 4900 J / 10 s = 490 W. Therefore, the power output decreases as the time increases."
}', '{"question": "Recall the formula for calculating power. Use this formula to determine the power output of a 50 kg student climbing a 10 m ladder in 5 seconds. Explain how the power output would change if the time was doubled.", "answer": "The formula for power is P = W/t, where W is work done and t is time. Work done W = mgh = 50 kg × 9.8 m/s² × 10 m = 4900 J. Power P = 4900 J / 5 s = 980 W. If the time is doubled to 10 seconds, the power output would be P = 4900 J / 10 s = 490 W. Therefore, the power output decreases as the time increases.", "reasoning": "The question requires recalling the formula for power and applying it in a scenario. The power output is calculated as P = W/t, where W is work done and t is time. Work done W = mgh = 50 kg × 9.8 m/s² × 10 m = 4900 J. Power P = 4900 J / 5 s = 980 W. If the time is doubled to 10 seconds, the power output would be P = 4900 J / 10 s = 490 W. Therefore, the power output decreases as the time increases."}']

