['{
  "question": "A worker pushes a box with a force of 50 N over a distance of 10 m. Calculate the work done. If the worker pushes the box with the same force but over a distance of 15 m, explain why the work done increases. Compare this scenario to pushing the box with a force of 60 N over 10 m. Which scenario results in more work? Justify your answer.",
  "answer": "For the first scenario, W = 50 N \u00d7 10 m = 500 J. For the second scenario, W = 50 N \u00d7 15 m = 750 J. For the third scenario, W = 60 N \u00d7 10 m = 600 J. The work done increases because work is directly proportional to distance when force is constant. Comparing the scenarios, pushing with 50 N over 15 m results in more work (750 J) than pushing with 60 N over 10 m (600 J). This shows that increasing either force or distance increases work, but in this case, increasing distance has a greater impact.",
  "reasoning": "The final question requires evaluating different scenarios and justifying the reasoning, aligning with Evaluate and DOK2."
}', '{"question": "A worker pushes a box with a force of 50 N over a distance of 10 m. Calculate the work done. If the worker pushes the box with the same force but over a distance of 15 m, explain why the work done increases. Compare this scenario to pushing the box with a force of 60 N over 10 m. Which scenario results in more work? Justify your answer.", "answer": "For the first scenario, W = 50 N × 10 m = 500 J. For the second scenario, W = 50 N × 15 m = 750 J. For the third scenario, W = 60 N × 10 m = 600 J. The work done increases because work is directly proportional to distance when force is constant. Comparing the scenarios, pushing with 50 N over 15 m results in more work (750 J) than pushing with 60 N over 10 m (600 J). This shows that increasing either force or distance increases work, but in this case, increasing distance has a greater impact.", "reasoning": "The final question requires evaluating different scenarios and justifying the reasoning, aligning with Evaluate and DOK2."}']

