  "answer": "A car traveling at a constant speed on a straight road is an example of Newton's first law in action. According to Newton's first law, an object in motion will stay in motion unless acted upon by an external force. In this case, the car continues to move at a constant velocity because the forces acting on it (such as the engine's forward thrust and air resistance) are balanced, resulting in no net force. This balance of forces means there is no acceleration, and thus the car maintains its constant velocity.",
