['{
  "question": "Recall the components of projectile motion and explain why the vertical component of velocity becomes zero at the maximum height. Using the given initial velocity of 30 m/s at 45 degrees, calculate the time to reach maximum height and the horizontal distance traveled during this time. Justify your calculations by referencing the principles of kinematics.",
  "answer": "The components of projectile motion include horizontal and vertical motion. The vertical component of velocity becomes zero at the maximum height because the projectile stops moving upward and begins to fall back down due to gravity. Using the initial velocity of 30 m/s at 45 degrees, the vertical component is v_y = 30 * sin(45) \u2248 21.21 m/s. The time to reach maximum height is when v_y = 0, which is t = 21.21 / 9.8 \u2248 2.16 seconds. The horizontal distance traveled is x = v_x * t, where v_x = 30 * cos(45) \u2248 21.21 m/s, so x \u2248 21.21 * 2.16 \u2248 45.8 meters. This is justified by the principles of kinematics, which state that the vertical motion is influenced by gravity, causing the vertical velocity to decrease until it reaches zero at the maximum height.",
  "reasoning": "The question now requires recalling the components of projectile motion, explaining the physical reasoning, and justifying the calculations using kinematic principles. This meets the DOK4 requirement as it involves higher-order thinking and comprehensive understanding of the concepts."
}', '{"question": "Recall the components of projectile motion and explain why the vertical component of velocity becomes zero at the maximum height. Using the given initial velocity of 30 m/s at 45 degrees, calculate the time to reach maximum height and the horizontal distance traveled during this time. Justify your calculations by referencing the principles of kinematics.", "answer": "The components of projectile motion include horizontal and vertical motion. The vertical component of velocity becomes zero at the maximum height because the projectile stops moving upward and begins to fall back down due to gravity. Using the initial velocity of 30 m/s at 45 degrees, the vertical component is v_y = 30 * sin(45) ≈ 21.21 m/s. The time to reach maximum height is when v_y = 0, which is t = 21.21 / 9.8 ≈ 2.16 seconds. The horizontal distance traveled is x = v_x * t, where v_x = 30 * cos(45) ≈ 21.21 m/s, so x ≈ 21.21 * 2.16 ≈ 45.8 meters. This is justified by the principles of kinematics, which state that the vertical motion is influenced by gravity, causing the vertical velocity to decrease until it reaches zero at the maximum height.", "reasoning": "The question now requires recalling the components of projectile motion, explaining the physical reasoning, and justifying the calculations using kinematic principles. This meets the DOK4 requirement as it involves higher-order thinking and comprehensive understanding of the concepts."}']

