['{
  "question": "Explain how the amount of thermal energy in an object depends on its mass when the temperature is constant.",
  "answer": "The amount of thermal energy in an object depends directly on its mass when the temperature is constant. This is because thermal energy is the total kinetic energy of the particles in an object, and more massive objects have more particles. Therefore, if two objects have the same temperature but different masses, the object with the greater mass will have more thermal energy. This relationship can be expressed as E = mc\u0394T, where E is the thermal energy, m is the mass, c is the specific heat capacity, and \u0394T is the change in temperature. Since the temperature is the same, the thermal energy is directly proportional to the mass.",
  "reasoning": "The final question requires understanding the relationship between thermal energy, mass, and temperature. It also requires explaining the reasoning behind the relationship, which aligns with the DOK4 level of cognitive demand. The answer provides a clear explanation of the relationship and uses the formula E = mc\u0394T to support the explanation."
}', '{"question": "Explain how the amount of thermal energy in an object depends on its mass when the temperature is constant.", "answer": "The amount of thermal energy in an object depends directly on its mass when the temperature is constant. This is because thermal energy is the total kinetic energy of the particles in an object, and more massive objects have more particles. Therefore, if two objects have the same temperature but different masses, the object with the greater mass will have more thermal energy. This relationship can be expressed as E = mcΔT, where E is the thermal energy, m is the mass, c is the specific heat capacity, and ΔT is the change in temperature. Since the temperature is the same, the thermal energy is directly proportional to the mass.", "reasoning": "The final question requires understanding the relationship between thermal energy, mass, and temperature. It also requires explaining the reasoning behind the relationship, which aligns with the DOK4 level of cognitive demand. The answer provides a clear explanation of the relationship and uses the formula E = mcΔT to support the explanation."}']

