['{
  "question": "Compare the arrangement and movement of particles in solids and liquids, and explain how these differences affect their physical properties.",
  "answer": "In solids, particles are tightly packed in a fixed arrangement and vibrate in place, which gives solids a definite shape and volume. In liquids, particles are close together but can move past one another, allowing liquids to flow and take the shape of their container while maintaining a definite volume. These differences in particle behavior explain why solids keep their shape and liquids do not.",
  "reasoning": "The question requires students to compare and analyze the arrangement and movement of particles in solids and liquids, which aligns with Bloom's Analyze level and DOK3 as it involves reasoning, comparison, and explanation."
}', '{"question": "Compare the arrangement and movement of particles in solids and liquids, and explain how these differences affect their physical properties.", "answer": "In solids, particles are tightly packed in a fixed arrangement and vibrate in place, which gives solids a definite shape and volume. In liquids, particles are close together but can move past one another, allowing liquids to flow and take the shape of their container while maintaining a definite volume. These differences in particle behavior explain why solids keep their shape and liquids do not.", "reasoning": "The question requires students to compare and analyze the arrangement and movement of particles in solids and liquids, which aligns with Bloom's Analyze level and DOK3 as it involves reasoning, comparison, and explanation."}']

['{
  "question": "A science teacher has two beakers, Beaker A and Beaker B. Beaker A contains 100 mL of water with 10 g of salt dissolved in it. Beaker B contains 150 mL of water with 20 g of salt dissolved in it. The teacher mixes the contents of both beakers into a larger container. What is the concentration of salt in the final mixture, and how does it compare to the concentration in each original beaker? Explain your reasoning and evaluate how the concentration would change if the volume of Beaker A was doubled. Design an experiment to determine the effect of changing the volume of the solvent on the final concentration. Evaluate the impact of changing the volume of the solvent versus changing the amount of solute on the final concentration. Justify your experimental design. Predict the outcome of the experiment based on your understanding of concentration. Explain why the predicted outcome is consistent with the concept of concentration. How does this relate to real-world applications of concentration?",
  "answer": "The final mixture has a total volume of 250 mL and a total mass of salt of 30 g. The concentration of salt in the final mixture is 30 g / 250 mL = 0.12 g/mL. This concentration is higher than the concentration in Beaker A (10 g / 100 mL = 0.1 g/mL) but lower than the concentration in Beaker B (20 g / 150 mL \u2248 0.133 g/mL). The final concentration is a weighted average of the two original concentrations based on their volumes. If the volume of Beaker A was doubled to 200 mL, the total volume would be 350 mL and the total mass of salt would be 30 g. The new concentration would be 30 g / 350 mL \u2248 0.0857 g/mL, which is lower than the original concentration. To design an experiment, the student could vary the volume of water in Beaker A while keeping the amount of salt constant, mix the contents, and measure the concentration of the final mixture. This would allow them to observe how changes in the volume of the solvent affect the final concentration. Changing the volume of the solvent affects the concentration by diluting the solution, while changing the amount of solute affects the concentration by increasing the amount of solute in the same volume of solvent. The experimental design is justified because it allows the student to control variables and observe the relationship between the volume of the solvent and the final concentration. Based on the understanding of concentration, the student can predict that increasing the volume of the solvent will decrease the concentration, while increasing the amount of solute will increase the concentration. The predicted outcome is consistent with the concept of concentration because concentration is defined as the amount of solute per unit volume of solution, so increasing the volume of the solvent while keeping the amount of solute constant will decrease the concentration, and increasing the amount of solute while keeping the volume of the solvent constant will increase the concentration. This relates to 