['{
  "question": "Design a model to explain how atoms combine to form molecules in a covalent bond, using water (H\u2082O) as an example. Include a diagram and a brief explanation of the bonding process.",
  "answer": "To model the formation of a water molecule (H\u2082O) through a covalent bond, start by representing two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom. Each hydrogen atom has one valence electron, while oxygen has six valence electrons. In a covalent bond, atoms share electrons to achieve a stable configuration. The oxygen atom shares one electron with each hydrogen atom, forming two single covalent bonds. This sharing allows both hydrogen atoms to achieve a full outer shell (like helium) and oxygen to achieve a full octet (like neon). The resulting structure is a bent shape due to the lone pairs on oxygen. Diagram: Draw two hydrogen atoms connected to an oxygen atom with single lines (representing shared electron pairs), and include two lone pairs on the oxygen. Explanation: Water forms when oxygen shares electrons with two hydrogen atoms, creating a stable molecule with a bent geometry.",
  "reasoning": "The question requires students to create a model of covalent bonding in water, which aligns with the Create level of Bloom's taxonomy and DOK3 complexity. It involves applying knowledge of atomic structure and bonding to construct a detailed explanation and diagram."
}', '{"question": "Design a model to explain how atoms combine to form molecules in a covalent bond, using water (H₂O) as an example. Include a diagram and a brief explanation of the bonding process.", "answer": "To model the formation of a water molecule (H₂O) through a covalent bond, start by representing two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom. Each hydrogen atom has one valence electron, while oxygen has six valence electrons. In a covalent bond, atoms share electrons to achieve a stable configuration. The oxygen atom shares one electron with each hydrogen atom, forming two single covalent bonds. This sharing allows both hydrogen atoms to achieve a full outer shell (like helium) and oxygen to achieve a full octet (like neon). The resulting structure is a bent shape due to the lone pairs on oxygen. Diagram: Draw two hydrogen atoms connected to an oxygen atom with single lines (representing shared electron pairs), and include two lone pairs on the oxygen. Explanation: Water forms when oxygen shares electrons with two hydrogen atoms, creating a stable molecule with a bent geometry.", "reasoning": "The question requires students to create a model of covalent bonding in water, which aligns with the Create level of Bloom's taxonomy and DOK3 complexity. It involves applying knowledge of atomic structure and bonding to construct a detailed explanation and diagram."}']

['{
