['{
  "question": "Explain why the atomic radius decreases across a period from left to right in the periodic table.",
  "answer": "The atomic radius decreases across a period from left to right because the number of protons in the nucleus increases, leading to a stronger positive charge. This increased nuclear charge pulls the electrons closer to the nucleus, reducing the atomic radius. Additionally, electrons are added to the same energy level, so there is no significant increase in shielding effect.",
  "reasoning": "Generated based on periodic trends and aligned with Bloom's Understand level and DOK2 cognitive demand."
}', '{"question": "Explain why the atomic radius decreases across a period from left to right in the periodic table.", "answer": "The atomic radius decreases across a period from left to right because the number of protons in the nucleus increases, leading to a stronger positive charge. This increased nuclear charge pulls the electrons closer to the nucleus, reducing the atomic radius. Additionally, electrons are added to the same energy level, so there is no significant increase in shielding effect.", "reasoning": "Generated based on periodic trends and aligned with Bloom's Understand level and DOK2 cognitive demand."}']

['{
  "question": "Explain what a compound is and how it differs from an elementary substance, using the molecular models to support your explanation and analyzing the types of atoms present in each.",
  "answer": "A compound is a substance formed when two or more different types of atoms are chemically bonded together. An elementary substance consists of only one type of atom. For example, CO2 (carbon dioxide) is a compound because it contains carbon and oxygen atoms bonded together, while O2 (oxygen gas) is an elementary substance because it consists of only oxygen atoms. The key difference is that compounds have different types of atoms, whereas elementary substances have only one type. Using the molecular models, we can see that the compound has multiple types of atoms, while the elementary substance has only one type. Analyzing the types of atoms present in each, we can see that the compound has a combination of different atoms, while the elementary substance has only one type.",
  "reasoning": "The question requires students to explain the concept of a compound and differentiate it from an elementary substance, using the molecular models to support their explanation and analyzing the types of atoms present in each, which aligns with understanding (Bloom's) and DOK3 as it involves explaining and comparing concepts."
