['{
  "question": "A chemist is studying the following equilibrium reaction:\n\n$$\n\\text{N}_2(g) + 3\\text{H}_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2\\text{NH}_3(g)\n$$\n\nThe system is initially at equilibrium with the following concentrations:\n\n- $[\\text{N}_2] = 0.5 \\, \\text{M}$\n- $[\\text{H}_2] = 1.5 \\, \\text{M}$\n- $[\\text{NH}_3] = 1.0 \\, \\text{M}$\n\nIf the concentration of $\\text{H}_2$ is suddenly increased to $2.5 \\, \\text{M}$, predict the direction in which the equilibrium will shift.\n\nAnswer:",
  "answer": "When the concentration of $\\text{H}_2$ is increased, the equilibrium will shift to the right (toward the products) to counteract the change. According to Le Chatelier's principle, the system will adjust to reduce the disturbance. By shifting to the right, more $\\text{NH}_3$ will be formed, consuming some of the excess $\\text{H}_2$. This shift helps restore equilibrium by reducing the concentration of $\\text{H}_2$ back toward its original value.",
  "reasoning": "This question requires students to apply their understanding of Le Chatelier's principle to predict the direction of equilibrium shift when a reactant concentration is increased. It involves analyzing the effect of a disturbance on a system at equilibrium and explaining the reasoning behind the shift, which aligns with the Apply level of Bloom's taxonomy and DOK3 cognitive demand."
