['{
  "question": "In a laboratory setting, a student is given 1-bromobutane and sodium cyanide in a polar aprotic solvent. Predict the major product of the SN2 reaction and explain the mechanism step-by-step, including the role of the solvent and the impact of steric hindrance.",
  "answer": "The major product is pentanenitrile (CH3CH2CH2CH2CN). The mechanism involves a backside attack by the cyanide ion (CN-) on the carbon atom bonded to the bromine, leading to the displacement of the bromide ion. This results in the formation of pentanenitrile with inversion of configuration at the carbon center. The choice of a polar aprotic solvent increases the reaction rate because it stabilizes the nucleophile without solvating it, making the nucleophile more reactive. Steric hindrance around the electrophilic carbon can slow down the reaction, but in this case, the primary alkyl halide has minimal steric hindrance, allowing the SN2 mechanism to proceed rapidly.",
